MarDRe Changelog
----------------
This file briefly describes the main changes for each version of
the MarDRe software package.

MarDRe v2.0 - 2026/10/25
------------------------
A major release. It requires Java 11 and Hadoop 3, and it changes which
read is kept among duplicates and the names written to the output (see
the notes about compatibility below)

	- Requirements: Java 11 or above, Hadoop 3.3.6 and the Hadoop Sequence
	Parser (HSP) 2.0.0 library. Tested with Java 11, 17 and 21

	- MarDRe is now also hosted on GitHub, where its source code and its
	releases are available: https://github.com/UDC-GAC/MarDRe. The website
	of MarDRe is https://mardre.des.udc.es

	- New option -d to remove only the optical duplicates: duplicate reads
	of the same tile whose distance is at most the given Euclidean distance.
	The X:Y coordinates are taken from the read names, either in the Illumina
	format or with the identifier of the SRA before them. Read names are not
	trimmed in this mode (see below)

	- FASTA datasets are now processed correctly, single-end or paired-end
	and compressed or not (the version of HSP used by 1.4 failed to read them)

	- No limit on the length of the reads. Previous versions failed with
	reads of more than about 2000 bases, and compared as empty the reads of
	more than 32767 bases

	- Overall performance is improved: with datasets of 12 million reads
	the CPU time of the tasks is between 8% and 16% lower than in 1.4 (when
	the names are trimmed as in 1.4), thanks to reading the records from
	their bytes, a faster serialization and sort of the intermediate data
	and a faster writing of the reads

	- The diagnostics of the program are reported through the SLF4J API,
	the final summary and timings are still printed on the standard output,
	the errors that end the execution are also printed on the standard error
	and the reasons why the tasks of a failed job failed are reported by
	the program

	- Added a test suite that includes the execution of the whole tool on
	the local runner of Hadoop

Compatibility with previous versions

	- The read that is kept among duplicates and near-duplicates is the one
	with the highest average quality, and the one with the lowest name if the
	average quality is the same. MarDRe 1.4 kept the one with the lowest
	average quality most of the times. The same applies to the in-mapper
	combiner

	- The names of the reads are no longer trimmed as HSP 1.x did (it cut
	them at their first space), so the output keeps the whole name line, as
	ParDRe does. The previous behavior can be restored with the generic
	Hadoop option -Dhsp.trim.sequence.name=true, but it must not be used
	with -d

	- With -m greater than 0 and with -d, each read is compared with the reads
	already kept, and a better duplicate replaces the one that was kept.
	Therefore, the result depends on the order in which the reads arrive at
	the reducers and some near-duplicates (about 0.01% of the reads) can
	remain in the output. With -m 0 the reads kept are always the same

	- The reads are not written in the order of the input dataset, as
	before, and the order inside each group of reads with the same prefix
	can differ from the one of previous versions

	- Paired-end datasets compressed with BZip2 are read in a single split
	by HSP, which can be slower than in previous versions

	- MARDRE_HOME is only needed by the program that launches the job, not by
	the MapReduce tasks, and the property dfs.blocksize is no longer required

Bug fixes

	- Fix the selection of the read with the highest average quality, which
	used a stale average quality and never compared the first read of each
	group

	- Fix the bases of the prefix written to the output with -l greater than
	22, which were wrong for most of the reads

	- Fix a failure in paired-end mode with -c when the reads have different
	lengths

	- Fix the state shared between the tasks of the same JVM, so several
	tasks can run at the same time in it

	- Fix the messages of the -nr warning and of the error of the parameter
	HDFS_BASE_PATH


MarDRe v1.4 - 2019/01/23
------------------------
A point release for the MarDRe 1.x line

	- Improved overall performance by optimizing the writing of Sequence objects

	- Disable merge operation by default

	- Fix typos in user's guide

	- Fix typos in the help usage of the program when using -v


MarDRe v1.3 - 2017/12/19
------------------------
A point release for the MarDRe 1.x line

	- MarDRe now uses the Hadoop Sequence Parser (HSP)* library to read
	input FASTQ/FASTA datasets. Overall performance is improved due to the
	custom sequence record readers provided by this library, especially in
	the paired-end mode when using a map-side join. Using HSP, this mode
	now executes a single MapReduce job (note that paired-end mode using a
	reduce-side join still needs to execute two MapReduce jobs)

		* HSP is available at https://github.com/UDC-GAC/hsp

	- Fix bug in paired-end mode scenarios


MarDRe v1.2 - 2017/07/28
------------------------
A point release for the MarDRe 1.x line

	- Optimized paired-end mode using a map-side join (enabled by default)

	- Support for input/output datasets compressed with Gzip (.gz extension)
	and BZip2 (.bz2 extension) codecs. Read user's guide for more information
	
	- New command-line options (-q and -f) to specify the input file format
	for compressed datasets

	- The user can decide to compare only a certain number of bases for each
	read using a new command-line option (-c)
	
	- The number of reducers can now be specified using a new command-line 
	option (-nr)
		
	- New configuration parameter to enable Snappy compression of the map
	output phase
	
	- New configuration parameter to specify HDFS block replication factor 
	for output sequence files
	
	- New configuration parameter to specify the HDFS base path used by MarDRe
	to store both the output and intermediate files
	
	- Fix bug in paired-end mode when processing FASTA datasets


MarDRe v1.1 - 2017/05/15
------------------------
A point release for the MarDRe 1.x line

	- Overall performance enhancements by reducing memory footprint, GC overhead
	and sequence parsing
	
	- Optimized paired-end mode

	- When two FASTQ reads are collapsed, now the one with the highest average 
	quality is kept

	- The user can specify the output file names using new command-line 
	options (-o and -r)
	
	- Sanity checks for the value of some configuration parameters
	
	- Minor bug fixes


MarDRe v1.0.1 - 2017/03/29
--------------------------
A point release for the MarDRe 1.x line

	- User's guide has been updated to specify that the current version of MarDRe 
	does not take into account the quality scores for selecting the representative 
	sequence when duplicated

	- Fix bug with the distribution tarball to include the parent directory


MarDRe v1.0 - 2017/02/01
------------------------
The first public release of MarDRe
